{"classified_as":[{"_label":"Collection Item","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404024","type":"Type"}],"id":"https://data.okeeffemuseum.org/touring-exhibition/868","identified_by":[{"classified_as":[{"_label":"preferred term","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670"}],"content":"Josephine Halvorson","type":"Name"}],"produced_by":{"carried_out_by":[{"_label":"Georgia O'Keeffe Museum","id":"actor/1","type":"Actor"}],"timespan":{"type":"TimeSpan"},"type":"Production"},"referred_to_by":[{"classified_as":[{"_label":"description statement","id":"https://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416"}],"content":"This exhibition features paintings by Josephine Halvorson (b. 1981),the Georgia O’Keeffe Museum’s first artist-in-residence. Alongside two works by Georgia O’Keeffe, this gallery features eleven paintings by Halvorson made mainly at O’Keeffe’s Ghost Ranch house, fifty miles northwest of Santa Fe. \nLike O’Keeffe, Halvorson practices close looking; both in nature, and during her residency, at the historic house which is preserved much as it was at the end of O’Keeffe’s life. \nHalvorson used gouche, an opaque, water-soluble medium that is sometimes called poster paint, giving the images a graphic quality. The paintings’ frames are a concrete-like mixture of resin, color, and earth collected at the site by the artist. The rulers screen-printed on the largest paintings evoke Halvorson’s attention to scale—the one-to-one relationship of painting and subject. \nWith the exception of the cow’s skull, Halvorson foregoes O’Keeffe’s more familiar motifs, focusing instead on the intimacy of an open cupboard or a well-worn stump-turned-stool.","type":"LinguisticObject"}],"representation":[],"type":"Activity"}